This lesson is about **16 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the CSS introduction on freeCodeCamp
- Create `styles.css`, link it from your page and change the colours and font

## Understand it

CSS (Cascading Style Sheets) is a list of **rules**. Each rule has a **selector** that picks elements, and **declarations** that say how they should look:

```css
h1 {
  color: #16233f;
  font-size: 2rem;
}
.card {
  background: white;
  padding: 16px;
}
#site-title {
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}
```

The three selectors you will use most: an **element selector** (`h1`) styles every h1; a **class selector** (`.card`) styles every element with `class="card"`; an **id selector** (`#site-title`) styles the one element with that id. Prefer classes: they can be reused, and ids are better kept for links and labels.

Put your CSS in its own file and link it from the head with `<link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css">`. Keeping structure (HTML) and appearance (CSS) apart means you can restyle a whole site by changing one file — and the browser downloads that file once and reuses it on every page.

## Key words

- **Selector:** the part of a rule that chooses which elements to style
- **Declaration:** one property and its value, like color: red;
- **Class:** a reusable name you give elements so CSS can style them together
- **Stylesheet:** a .css file of rules linked from your HTML

## Read and watch

- [CSS basics](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/Getting_started_with_the_web/CSS_basics) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [CSS selectors](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_selectors) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Learn CSS](https://web.dev/learn/css) — web.dev (Google)

