This lesson is about **50 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the Styling Forms workshop on freeCodeCamp
- Style your sign-up form so it is easy to use on a phone

## Understand it

An unstyled form works but looks unfinished and is hard to use on a phone. Styling it well is mostly about clarity:

```css
label { display: block; font-weight: 600; margin-bottom: 4px; }
input, select, textarea {
  width: 100%;
  min-height: 44px;
  padding: 10px 12px;
  font: inherit;               /* forms do not inherit fonts by default */
  border: 1px solid #8a8f98;
  border-radius: 8px;
}
input:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #f5b026; outline-offset: 2px; }
input:user-invalid { border-color: #b42318; }
```

The principles behind those lines: labels sit **above** fields (they stay visible and fit narrow screens); fields are full width and at least 44px tall (a finger is not a mouse pointer); focus is **obvious**, so keyboard users know where they are — never remove the outline without replacing it; and errors are shown next to the field in words, not only with a red border.

Group related fields with spacing, put the submit button where the eye ends, and label it with what it does: "Send my application" beats "Submit".

## Key words

- **:focus-visible:** styles the element the keyboard is on
- **:user-invalid:** styles a field the user has filled in wrongly
- **Tap target:** the area you can press; aim for at least 44 by 44 pixels

## Read and watch

- [Styling web forms](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/Forms/Styling_web_forms) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
