## What to do

- Finish the CSS Grid workshops on freeCodeCamp
- Build a photo gallery that goes from one column on a phone to four on a laptop

## Understand it

One line makes a gallery that fits any screen with no media queries at all:

```css
.gallery {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(160px, 1fr));
  gap: 12px;
}
```

Read it as: "make as many columns as fit, each at least 160px wide and sharing any spare room". On a 360px phone that is two columns; on a laptop, six or seven.

**Named areas** draw the layout in words:

```css
.page {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-areas: "head head" "side main" "foot foot";
  grid-template-columns: 220px 1fr;
}
header { grid-area: head; }
aside { grid-area: side; }
```

Redefine `grid-template-areas` in a media query and the whole page rearranges without touching the HTML.

**Grid or Flexbox?** A good rule: Flexbox when the *content* should decide the sizes and things flow in a line (a nav bar, tags, buttons); Grid when the *layout* should decide and things line up in two directions (a page, a gallery, a form with aligned labels). Most real pages use both.

## Key words

- **auto-fill:** makes as many columns as will fit
- **minmax():** a size between a minimum and a maximum
- **grid-template-areas:** names regions of a grid so a layout can be drawn in words

## Read and watch

- [Grid template areas](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_grid_layout/Grid_template_areas) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
