This lesson is about **45 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the first CSS Animation workshop on freeCodeCamp (to about step 45)

## Understand it

A **transition** animates the change between two states. You say which property, how long, and how it speeds up:

```css
.button {
  background: #16233f;
  transition: background-color 200ms ease, transform 200ms ease;
}
.button:hover, .button:focus-visible {
  background: #2a3b63;
  transform: translateY(-2px);
}
```

A **keyframe animation** runs by itself, through steps you define:

```css
@keyframes pulse {
  0%   { transform: scale(1); }
  50%  { transform: scale(1.08); }
  100% { transform: scale(1); }
}
.badge { animation: pulse 1.5s ease-in-out infinite; }
```

Animate `transform` and `opacity` where you can: the browser can move and fade things cheaply, whereas animating `width`, `height` or `top` makes it recalculate the layout every frame, which stutters on cheaper phones.

Good movement has a **purpose**: it shows that something changed, where something came from, or that the page is working. 150–300ms feels responsive; longer feels slow. Movement for its own sake distracts — and remember the reduced-motion setting from last lesson.

## Key words

- **Transition:** a smooth change from one style to another when a state changes
- **@keyframes:** named steps of an animation
- **Easing:** how an animation speeds up and slows down, like ease-in-out
- **transform:** moves, scales or rotates an element without changing the layout

## Read and watch

- [Using CSS transitions](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_transitions/Using_CSS_transitions) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Using CSS animations](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/CSS_animations/Using_CSS_animations) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
