This lesson is about **25 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the Loops lessons on freeCodeCamp

## Understand it

A **loop** repeats work so you do not have to write it out:

```js
// for: when you need a counter
for (let i = 1; i <= 5; i++) {
  console.log(`Round ${i}`);
}

// for...of: once for each item in an array
for (const course of courses) {
  console.log(course);
}

// while: until a condition stops being true
let attempts = 0;
while (attempts < 3 && !passed) {
  attempts++;
}
```

The `for` loop has three parts: start (`let i = 1`), keep going while (`i <= 5`), and step (`i++`). Prefer `for...of` for arrays — it cannot go off the end.

`break` leaves a loop early (you found what you were looking for); `continue` skips to the next round.

The danger is an **infinite loop**: a `while` whose condition never becomes false freezes the tab. Before you run one, ask: what changes each time round, and will that eventually make the condition false? If the browser hangs, close the tab — nothing is broken permanently.

## Key words

- **Loop:** code that repeats
- **for...of:** a loop over every item in an array
- **break / continue:** leave a loop / skip to its next round
- **Infinite loop:** a loop that never stops because its condition stays true

## Read and watch

- [Looping code](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/JavaScript/Building_blocks/Looping_code) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Loops: while and for](https://javascript.info/while-for) — javascript.info

