This lesson is about **36 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the DOM Manipulation and Events workshop on freeCodeCamp
- Make a button that counts how many times it is pressed

## Understand it

An **event** is something that happens on the page: a click, a key press, a form being sent. You attach a **listener** — a function that runs when it happens:

```js
const button = document.querySelector("#count");
let presses = 0;

button.addEventListener("click", () => {
  presses++;
  button.textContent = `Pressed ${presses} times`;
});

const form = document.querySelector("form");
form.addEventListener("submit", (event) => {
  event.preventDefault();   // stop the page reloading
  const name = form.elements.name.value.trim();
  console.log("Hello", name);
});
```

The listener receives an **event object** with details: which element (`event.target`), which key (`event.key`), and methods such as `preventDefault()`, which stops the browser's normal action.

Useful events: `click`, `input` (every keystroke in a field), `change`, `submit`, `keydown`. Listen for `submit` on the form, not `click` on the button — that way pressing Enter works too.

A `<button>` fires `click` from the keyboard as well. A `<div>` with a click listener does not — another reason to use the right element.

## Key words

- **Event:** something that happens on the page, like a click
- **Event listener:** a function that runs when an event happens
- **preventDefault():** stops the browser's normal action for an event
- **event.target:** the element the event happened on

## Read and watch

- [Introduction to events](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/JavaScript/Building_blocks/Events) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [EventTarget.addEventListener()](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/EventTarget/addEventListener) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)

