This lesson is about **50 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the first Form Validation workshop on freeCodeCamp

## Understand it

Validation means checking input before using it, and saying clearly what to fix. Good validation is kind: it helps people succeed rather than scolding them.

HTML gives you a lot for free — `required`, `type="email"`, `minlength`, `pattern` — and the browser shows a message. JavaScript lets you go further:

```js
const form = document.querySelector("#signup");
const phone = form.elements.phone;
const msg = document.querySelector("#phone-error");

form.addEventListener("submit", (e) => {
  const value = phone.value.replace(/\s/g, "");
  if (!/^0[789][01]\d{8}$/.test(value)) {
    e.preventDefault();
    msg.textContent = "Enter an 11-digit Nigerian number, like 0803 123 4567.";
    phone.setAttribute("aria-invalid", "true");
    phone.focus();
  }
});
```

Rules for good error messages: say **what** is wrong and **how** to fix it, with an example; put the message **next to the field**; link it with `aria-describedby` so screen readers read it; and do not clear what the person typed. "Invalid input" helps nobody.

Be generous in what you accept — strip spaces and dashes from a phone number instead of rejecting "0803 123 4567".

## Key words

- **Validation:** checking input is acceptable before using it
- **aria-invalid:** tells assistive technology a field has an error
- **aria-describedby:** links a field to the text that explains it, such as an error

## Read and watch

- [Client-side form validation](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/Forms/Form_validation) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Validate input](https://www.w3.org/WAI/tutorials/forms/validation/) — W3C Web Accessibility Initiative

