## What to do

- Do the Audio and Video events lessons on freeCodeCamp
- **With an AI assistant —** Use an AI assistant as a tutor: ask it to explain an error message line by line, then fix the bug yourself. Ask "why" until you could explain it to a classmate.

## Understand it

The `<audio>` and `<video>` elements play media with built-in controls. JavaScript can drive them and react to what they do:

```js
const video = document.querySelector("video");
const btn = document.querySelector("#play");

btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
  video.paused ? video.play() : video.pause();
});
video.addEventListener("play",  () => btn.textContent = "Pause");
video.addEventListener("pause", () => btn.textContent = "Play");
video.addEventListener("ended", () => console.log("Finished"));
video.addEventListener("timeupdate", () => {
  progress.value = video.currentTime / video.duration;
});
```

Useful properties: `currentTime` (where you are, in seconds), `duration`, `volume` (0 to 1), `muted`, `playbackRate` (1.5 for faster).

Browsers block media that plays sound by itself until the visitor has interacted with the page — so start playback from a click. Add **captions** to video with a `<track>` element: they serve deaf viewers, people watching on a noisy bus, and anybody watching with the sound off, which on phones is most people.

## Key words

- **HTMLMediaElement:** the shared controls of audio and video elements
- **currentTime:** the playback position in seconds
- **Autoplay policy:** browsers block sound until the user interacts
- **Captions:** text of what is said, for people who cannot hear it

## Read and watch

- [Video and audio content](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/HTML/Multimedia_and_embedding/Video_and_audio_content) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
