## What to do

- Do the Classes lessons on freeCodeCamp

## Understand it

A **class** is a blueprint: describe a kind of thing once, then make as many objects from it as you need.

```js
class Learner {
  constructor(name, centre) {
    this.name = name;
    this.centre = centre;
    this.badges = [];
  }
  award(badge) {
    this.badges.push(badge);
    return `${this.name} earned ${badge}!`;
  }
}

const tobi = new Learner("Tobi", "Egbeda");
tobi.award("First website");
```

`constructor` runs once when you write `new Learner(...)`, setting up the new object. `this` means "the object being made or used". Methods written in the class are shared by every object made from it.

One class can **extend** another and add to it:

```js
class Mentor extends Learner {
  constructor(name, centre, mentees) {
    super(name, centre);   // run Learner's constructor first
    this.mentees = mentees;
  }
}
```

Classes shine when you have many things of the same kind that carry their own data and behaviour — cards in a game, items in a shop. For a handful of values, a plain object is simpler.

## Key words

- **Class:** a blueprint for making objects of one kind
- **Instance:** one object made from a class with new
- **constructor:** the method that sets up a new instance
- **this:** the object a method is working on

## Read and watch

- [Classes in JavaScript](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/JavaScript/Objects/Classes_in_JavaScript) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Class basic syntax](https://javascript.info/class) — javascript.info

