This lesson is about **40 freeCodeCamp steps**. Work through them in order — the steps build on each other.

## What to do

- Do the Asynchronous JavaScript lessons on freeCodeCamp
- Fetch data from a free public API and show it on your page

## Understand it

Getting data from a server takes time — maybe a second, maybe ten on a weak connection. If JavaScript stopped and waited, the page would freeze. Instead it works **asynchronously**: it starts the request, carries on, and handles the answer when it arrives.

A **promise** is a value that will arrive later. `async`/`await` lets you write promise code that reads top to bottom:

```js
async function loadJoke() {
  try {
    const res = await fetch("https://official-joke-api.appspot.com/random_joke");
    if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Server said ${res.status}`);
    const joke = await res.json();
    output.textContent = `${joke.setup} — ${joke.punchline}`;
  } catch (err) {
    output.textContent = "Couldn't load a joke. Check your connection and try again.";
  }
}
```

`fetch` sends the request; `await` waits for the response without freezing the page; `res.json()` reads the body as data. Note `fetch` only fails by itself if the network is down — a 404 or 500 still "succeeds", so check `res.ok` yourself.

Every screen that loads data has three states: **loading** (show that something is happening), **success**, and **error** with a way to try again. A blank space that might be loading or might be broken is the worst of all.

## Key words

- **Asynchronous:** work that finishes later, while the rest of the program carries on
- **Promise:** a value that will be available in the future
- **async / await:** a way to write promise code that reads top to bottom
- **fetch:** the browser's function for requesting data over the network

## Read and watch

- [How to use promises](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn/JavaScript/Asynchronous/Promises) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
- [Using the Fetch API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Fetch_API/Using_Fetch) — MDN Web Docs (Mozilla)
