## What to do

- Build an API for your to-do items: list, add, update and delete (keep data in an array for now)
- **With an AI assistant —** ask it to describe what a request or query does, then test it with sample data first. AI can invent functions that do not exist — check the official docs.

## Understand it

A **REST API** organises a server around **resources** (things, like tasks) and uses HTTP methods for what you do to them:

| Method | Path | Does | Success |
|---|---|---|---|
| GET | /api/tasks | list all | 200 |
| GET | /api/tasks/:id | read one | 200 |
| POST | /api/tasks | create | 201 |
| PATCH | /api/tasks/:id | update part | 200 |
| DELETE | /api/tasks/:id | delete | 204 |

```js
let tasks = [];
app.post("/api/tasks", (req, res) => {
  const task = { id: Date.now().toString(), title: req.body.title, done: false };
  tasks.push(task);
  res.status(201).json(task);
});
app.patch("/api/tasks/:id", (req, res) => {
  const task = tasks.find(t => t.id === req.params.id);
  if (!task) return res.status(404).json({ error: "not_found" });
  if (typeof req.body.done === "boolean") task.done = req.body.done;
  res.json(task);
});
```

Paths are **nouns**, plural (`/tasks`, not `/getTasks`); the method is the verb. Return the created or updated item so the client does not need a second request. Be consistent: if errors look like `{ "error": "..." }` once, they should everywhere.

## Key words

- **REST:** a style of API built around resources and HTTP methods
- **Resource:** a thing an API manages, like a task or a user
- **Endpoint:** one method and path of an API
- **201 Created:** the status code for a successful create

