## What to do

- Connect with mongoose.connect
- Change each route to use the Task model
- Restart the server and check your data is still there

## Understand it

Today the array in your API is replaced by the database. Connect once, when the server starts:

```js
await mongoose.connect(process.env.MONGODB_URI);
app.listen(3000);
```

Then change each route, one at a time, testing as you go:

```js
app.get("/api/tasks", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    res.json(await Task.find().sort({ createdAt: -1 }));
  } catch (err) { next(err); }
});

app.post("/api/tasks", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const task = await Task.create({ title: req.body.title });
    res.status(201).json(task);
  } catch (err) {
    if (err.name === "ValidationError") return res.status(400).json({ error: "invalid_task" });
    next(err);
  }
});
```

Two new failure cases appear. An id that is not a valid MongoDB id makes `findById` throw a **CastError** — answer 404, not 500. And the database might be unreachable — the error middleware catches that.

The proof: add a task, stop the server, start it again, list the tasks. It is still there. Your app now remembers — that is the line between a demo and a real application.

## Key words

- **Connection string:** the address and credentials used to reach a database
- **CastError:** Mongoose's error when a value cannot be the right type, such as a bad id
- **Persistence:** data surviving a restart

