## Understand it

Catch-up first: anything overdue, then your weakest topic, practised from memory.

The challenge — a **search route** — is a small feature that teaches several things at once:

```js
app.get("/api/tasks/search", async (req, res, next) => {
  try {
    const q = String(req.query.q || "").trim().slice(0, 50);
    if (q.length < 2) return res.status(400).json({ error: "query_too_short" });
    const safe = q.replace(/[.*+?^${}()|[\]\\]/g, "\\$&");   // escape regex characters
    const found = await Task.find({ title: new RegExp(safe, "i") }).limit(20);
    res.json(found);
  } catch (err) { next(err); }
});
```

Notice the habits: the query is turned into a string, trimmed and **limited** in length; anything shorter than two characters is refused; special regex characters are **escaped**, so a search for `c++` does not break the pattern; and results are **capped** at 20 so one search cannot return a million documents.

Route order matters: put `/api/tasks/search` **before** `/api/tasks/:id`, or Express will read "search" as an id. For large collections, a database **index** on the field makes searching fast — look up MongoDB text indexes if you want to go further.

## Key words

- **Query string:** values after ? in a URL, like ?q=css
- **Escaping:** making special characters be read literally
- **Index:** a database structure that makes lookups fast
- **Pagination / limit:** returning results in small pages instead of all at once

